<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Litani River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Litani is the longest river wholly within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, about 140 kilometres long. It rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek</a>, flows south between the Lebanon and Anti-Lebanon ranges, then turns west through a deep gorge below Beaufort Castle to reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. It is an important source of water and hydroelectric power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/litani-river.jpg" alt="Litani River" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sonia Rachid (Sonia Fares) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Litani_river_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Litani rises in the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek</a>, and empties into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. It exceeds 140 kilometres in length and has an average annual flow estimated at 0.92 cubic kilometres. Its basin covers 2,110 square kilometres, the largest watershed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, about 20 per cent of the country, and includes the marshes of Kafr Zabad and the Aammiq wetlands, which host nearly 250 bird species on a migration route. After flowing south parallel to the Syrian border the river bends westward, and this lower stretch is called the Qasimiyeh. Along it lies one of the largest irrigated areas of the country, where farmers grow mainly citrus and bananas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The river is called Leontes, 'lion river', in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> sources, and in medieval times Līṭa. Its name is thought to come from the Semitic sea serpent Ltn, a seven-headed servant of the sea god Yam, and the oldest reference to the name is in Papyrus Anastasi I. In antiquity the river marked boundaries between civilisations, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> and later Hellenistic and Roman authorities. The region was important for trade and agriculture, and irrigation systems were developed there in Roman times.</p><p>About 10 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> the river was crossed by the Roman Leontes Bridge, now in ruins. In June 1941 British commandos and Australian troops attacked Vichy French forces at the river's mouth in what is called the Battle of the Litani River.</p><p>From the early 1950s the river was seen as a basis for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>'s infrastructure, and the Litani River Authority was set up in 1954 with World Bank funding. After a first failed attempt, the dam was completed in stages from 1962 to 1966 under Henry Naccache, who had been appointed in 1960, and was named the Albert Naccache Dam after his father. It is 60 metres high and 1,350 metres long and created Lake Qaraoun. The 16-kilometre Awwali tunnel, whose first drilling in 1958 failed because of the difficult soil, was finished with the help of French experts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The works tap a drop of 850 metres between Lake Qaraoun and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and the generators produce a maximum of 185 megawatts, the largest hydroelectric project in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. The upper flows are diverted through tunnels, ponds and plants and meet the sea several kilometres north of the river's original mouth, so that the upper basin is hydrologically separate from the lower reaches. The river irrigates thousands of hectares and about 31 per cent of the income within the basin comes from agriculture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Litani River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yet since 2 March 2026 intensified Israeli attacks have killed over 1,000 people and displaced nearly a million, and Israel has announced a buffer zone up to the <b>Litani River</b> with the demolition of border villages.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lebanese-republic">Lebanese Republic · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The lordship included the coast between Beirut and Tyre and inland castles such as Beaufort above the <b>Litani</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-sidon">Lordship of Sidon</a></li></ul></section>
